<section id="premise">
  <h2>What kind of game is this?</h2>
  <p class="source-note">Page 12</p>
  <div class="grid-2"><div><p>You tell the story of <strong>one protagonist</strong> caught in a tale of English rural horror. They investigate a mystery while people, landscape, circumstance, and something uncanny close in around them.</p><p>Solo players write the story in a journal. Groups narrate the story aloud together, taking turns as <strong>Active Narrator</strong>.</p></div><div class="rule-box"><h3>Core idea</h3><p>Draw a card → turn it into a connected scene → roll if it is an obstacle → record or narrate the result → draw again.</p><p class="note">Each new scene should grow naturally from what happened before. The cards prompt the story; they do not replace it.</p></div></div>
</section>

<section id="need">
  <h2>What you need</h2>
  <p class="source-note">Page 10</p>
  <div class="grid-3">
    <div class="card"><span class="label">Required</span><ul class="checklist"><li>A scenario</li><li>A play sheet</li><li>A d10</li><li>Two colours of tokens</li><li>A 19-card Story Deck</li></ul></div>
    <div class="card"><span class="label">Solo</span><ul class="checklist"><li>A journal or loose paper</li><li>A quiet place to write</li><li>Candle or lamplight, optionally</li></ul></div>
    <div class="card"><span class="label">Group</span><ul class="checklist"><li>Components visible to everyone</li><li>A marker for the Active Narrator</li><li>Agreed safety tools / X-card</li></ul></div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="character">
  <h2>Create the protagonist</h2>
  <p class="source-note">Pages 12–13</p>
  <div class="grid-2"><div><div class="step"><div class="step-num">1</div><div><h3>Use the scenario as your brief</h3><p>It usually establishes the protagonist’s broad role, period, or circumstances.</p></div></div><div class="step"><div class="step-num">2</div><div><h3>Write the essentials</h3><p>Name, occupation, and a short background.</p></div></div><div class="step"><div class="step-num">3</div><div><h3>Add hooks for the fiction</h3><p>Choose <strong>three defining features</strong> and <strong>three fears</strong>. Features may be possessions, physical traits, habits, or personal details.</p></div></div></div><div><div class="card"><span class="label">Attributes</span><p>Split <span class="big-number">10 points</span> between <strong>Resolve</strong> and <strong>Spirit</strong>.</p><div class="equation">Resolve + Spirit = 10<br /><small>minimum 3 in each</small></div><p><span class="token resolve">R</span> <strong>Resolve</strong> is determination, courage, and the ability to rationalise the irrational.</p><p><span class="token spirit">S</span> <strong>Spirit</strong> is emotional and physical well-being. Low Spirit means horror, doubt, injury, madness, or worse.</p></div></div></div>
  <p class="note"><strong>Group play:</strong> everyone contributes details, but you are all telling the story of the same protagonist.</p>
</section>
